# Scoped clang-format config for this By-Example topic's runnable code (learning/code/**,
# learning/capstone/code/**).
#
# Why this file exists: every example in this topic carries a dense trailing `// => ...`
# educational annotation on nearly every code line (the by-example convention's annotation-density
# requirement). The repo has no root-level .clang-format config, so `clang-format -i` (run by the
# pre-commit format-staged gate) falls back to LLVM's default style, which wraps long annotated
# lines/macros across multiple physical lines and reorders #include directives alphabetically --
# both silently drift the on-disk .c files out of byte-identity with the markdown pages' embedded
# code blocks. See `../computer-science-foundations/ruff.toml` for the same fix applied to an
# earlier topic's Python content.
#
# ColumnLimit uses a large finite number (2000), NOT 0: clang-format treats `ColumnLimit: 0` as
# "never wrap, but also never re-join a line that already fits" -- an already-wrapped line stays
# wrapped even under this config, so it fails to undo damage from a prior run without this file. A
# large finite limit forces a genuine re-layout, joining every annotated statement onto one line.
# 2000 comfortably covers every example's longest merged code+comment line here, so
# `clang-format -i` stays a no-op (idempotent) on a second run. AlignEscapedNewlines defaults to
# `Right` in LLVM style, which right-pads every line of a multi-line macro's `\` continuation out
# to the ColumnLimit itself (2000 columns of trailing whitespace) -- `Left` aligns the `\` just
# past each macro's own longest line instead.
BasedOnStyle: LLVM
IndentWidth: 4
ColumnLimit: 2000
SortIncludes: false
AlignEscapedNewlines: Left
